31
00:01:40,133 --> 00:01:41,493
under deadline pressure.

32
00:01:41,493 --> 00:01:43,760
Notice, too, how short this actually is.

33
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:48,125
Four lines, four owners, no paragraph of explanation attached to any of them,

34
00:01:48,125 --> 00:01:52,773
because the explanation already happened, properly, across five separate lectures.

35
00:01:52,773 --> 00:01:57,307
A checklist that's short enough to read in thirty seconds is one people actually

36
00:01:57,307 --> 00:01:57,591
read.

37
00:01:57,591 --> 00:02:02,069
A checklist that reads like a policy document is one people learn to skip past.

38
00:02:02,069 --> 00:02:04,279
Notice the shape of each question, too.

39
00:02:04,279 --> 00:02:08,984
All four are yes-or-no, the same discipline Section 6.1 insisted on from the start.

40
00:02:08,984 --> 00:02:12,668
"Could each claim survive being said out loud" has a real answer.

41
00:02:12,668 --> 00:02:17,260
A vaguer version, "does this feel honest," doesn't, and a checklist full of vague

42
00:02:17,260 --> 00:02:20,774
questions gets skipped exactly as fast as no checklist at all.

43
00:02:21,324 --> 00:02:23,378
Notice this isn't a hypothetical run.

44
00:02:23,378 --> 00:02:27,931
Two of the four checks actually caught something real on Harlan's own account, the

45
00:02:27,931 --> 00:02:31,040
two you've already seen in full in Sections 6.2 and 6.4.

46
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:34,649
This checklist isn't a formality that happens to pass every time.

47
00:02:34,649 --> 00:02:39,202
It's the thing that actually caught two genuine problems before either one spent a

48
00:02:39,202 --> 00:02:39,590
dollar.

49
00:02:39,590 --> 00:02:42,311
Notice, too, which checks passed cleanly and why.

50
00:02:42,311 --> 00:02:46,642
Accuracy passed because Section 3's colour and text fixes were already applied

51
00:02:46,642 --> 00:02:48,974
before this batch ever reached pre-flight.

52
00:02:48,974 --> 00:02:49,862
That's not luck.

53
00:02:49,862 --> 00:02:54,081
It's what happens when the earlier sections' discipline is already built in:

54
00:02:54,081 --> 00:02:57,413
pre-flight catches what's left over, not everything at once.

55
00:02:57,413 --> 00:03:01,355
That's actually the healthiest way to think about this checklist's job.

56
00:03:01,355 --> 00:03:04,408
It isn't the only line of defence in this whole course.

57
00:03:04,408 --> 00:03:08,795
It's the last one, catching whatever slipped through every earlier check, brand

58
00:03:08,795 --> 00:03:13,403
control in Section 3, licence tiers in Section 4, the claims discipline you've been

59
00:03:13,403 --> 00:03:17,567
building since Section 2.3's hook taxonomy first taught you to be specific.

60
00:03:17,567 --> 00:03:22,120
Four checks here, not forty, because forty would mean the earlier sections weren't

61
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:23,008
doing their job.

62
00:03:23,558 --> 00:03:26,963
One honest expectation before you run this on your own batch.

63
00:03:26,963 --> 00:03:30,089
Passing zero for four on the first try would be unusual.

64
00:03:30,089 --> 00:03:34,778
Failing one or two, the way Harlan's batch did, is the normal result of a real check

65
00:03:34,778 --> 00:03:36,062
actually doing its job.

66
00:03:36,062 --> 00:03:40,360
Measure success the same way Section 3.6 and 4.6 already taught you to: not a

67
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:44,993
perfect first pass, but every real problem caught here, before Section seven's test

68
00:03:44,993 --> 00:03:46,947
finds it the expensive way instead.

69
00:03:46,947 --> 00:03:51,356
Ask yourself, honestly, what a zero-for-four result on your own first run would

70
00:03:51,356 --> 00:03:52,138
actually mean.

71
00:03:52,138 --> 00:03:56,548
Either your batch is genuinely cleaner than Harlan's, which is possible, or the

72
00:03:56,548 --> 00:04:00,511
checklist isn't being run with real scrutiny, which is far more common.

73
00:04:00,511 --> 00:04:04,809
If nothing ever fails, go back and check whether you're actually testing each

74
